+## Safest verification order
+
+### 1. Provision and isolate staging
+
+- [ ] Create separate staging frontend/API services, a staging-only PostgreSQL
+  project/database, a staging-only Redis instance, and a distinct Google OAuth
+  client. Restrict access to the staging environment and test users.
+- [ ] Verify network and credential isolation: staging API cannot connect to
+  production DB/Redis/storage; staging credentials cannot authenticate to
+  production; staging logs and backups are access-controlled.
+- [ ] Configure the frontend's `BACKEND_URL` to the staging API origin and
+  `SITE_URL` (if a custom origin is used) to the exact staging frontend origin.
+  Verify the frontend build uses no OAuth client secret or other provider
+  secret.
+- [ ] Set API environment values from the secret manager/deployment settings:
+  `VERVFY_ENVIRONMENT=staging`, `VERVFY_HTTPS_ONLY=1`,
+  `VERVFY_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=<verified proxy hop count>`,
+  `VERVFY_COOKIE_SAME_SITE=lax`, and a unique staging
+  `VERVFY_SECRET_KEY`. Keep `GOOGLE_OAUTH_ENABLED=0` throughout provisioning.
+- [ ] Configure `DATABASE_URL` for the staging application role and
+  `REDIS_URL` for staging Redis. Do not reuse the production `SUPABASE_URL`,
+  `SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY`, storage bucket, DB, or Redis. Only configure OAuth
+  credentials after migrations and the pre-enable checks below pass.
+- [ ] Confirm backup/snapshot and restore procedures for the staging DB.
+  Confirm staging Redis can be isolated, paused, and tested without affecting
+  any other service. Do not use `FLUSHALL` or induce memory pressure on shared
+  Redis.
+
+### 2. Register the staging Google client
+
+- [ ] Create a distinct OAuth 2.0 **Web application** client in the approved
+  Google Cloud project. Use the approved consent-screen audience/test-user
+  policy; do not add real users beyond the authorized staging testers.
+- [ ] Register this exact authorized redirect URI, replacing the placeholder
+  with the verified frontend origin:
+  `https://<STAGING_FRONTEND_HOST>/api/auth/google/callback`
+- [ ] Do not use wildcards, path variants, trailing-slash alternatives, the
+  API's private hostname, or the production URI. If Google requires an
+  authorized JavaScript origin for the selected client configuration, add
+  only `https://<STAGING_FRONTEND_HOST>`; never add a broad origin.
+- [ ] Enable the Google OIDC `auth_time` behavior described in the readiness
+  document for the staging client/project. Record the setting and verify the
+  actual returned claim during step 6; configuration alone is not evidence.
+- [ ] Store the client ID and secret only in the staging API secret store as
+  `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` and `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`. Set
+  `GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=https://<STAGING_FRONTEND_HOST>/api/auth/google/callback`
+  and `GOOGLE_FRONTEND_ORIGIN=https://<STAGING_FRONTEND_HOST>`. Do not expose
+  the secret through `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`, frontend build arguments, browser
+  responses, or logs.
+
+### 3. Prepare the staging database and apply migrations
+
+The release target is `0027_storage_lifecycle`; local source head 0028 must remain unapplied. Follow
+[the staging migration review](staging-alembic-review.md), including its guarded
+preflight/postflight commands. 0025 corrects cover-source values; 0026 supplies
+scoped runtime grants and owned-sequence USAGE. Both are forward-only. An empty
+staging database must apply all 26 revisions, including 0021–0024; never stamp
+past the historical migrations. These changes authorize no hosted execution.
+
+- [ ] Confirm this is the isolated staging database by checking project/database
+  identity using the DBA-approved read-only method. Confirm no production data
+  or production connections are present. Take and verify a restorable snapshot.
+- [ ] Review the complete migration chain from the database's current revision
+  through `0021_provider_identities`, `0022_provider_identity_lookup`,
+  `0023_missing_media_columns`, and `0024_provider_identity_runtime_access`.
+  The local PostgreSQL tests do not establish that the deployed database's
+  historical schema is at the expected revision.
+- [ ] Have the DBA provision `vervfy_identity_lookup_owner` and
+  `vervfy_identity_lookup` as dedicated `NOLOGIN NOINHERIT NOSUPERUSER
+  NOBYPASSRLS NOCREATEDB NOCREATEROLE NOREPLICATION` roles. The owner role must
+  have no role memberships; only the migration principal may be a member.
+  Audit pre-existing and `PUBLIC` privileges. Do not make either role a login.
+- [ ] Use a separate, least-privilege migration principal that owns the
+  relevant application tables, can alter `users` and create `provider_identities`,
+  is permitted to assume the lookup-owner role for 0022 function ownership,
+  and has database `CREATE` for the private schema. Confirm the role membership
+  and schema/search path before running any migration. Only this migration
+  principal may be a member of the lookup-owner role; membership must permit
+  `SET ROLE` but must not be inherited. Do not grant the migration principal
+  superuser, `BYPASSRLS`, or broad administrator access.
+- [ ] Apply migrations in order through 0027 (from base for empty staging), using the approved
+  staging migration mechanism and a transaction. In 0022, grant the executor
+  schema usage and function execution while the migration principal still owns
+  the function, then transfer function ownership to the lookup-owner role.
+  Never point it at production.
+  Stop if role preflight, schema/revision checks, or expected locks fail; do not
+  bypass preflight or grant superuser/BYPASSRLS as a shortcut.
+- [ ] Inspect resulting RLS and grants with the DBA: `provider_identities` must
+  retain ENABLE/FORCE RLS and tenant isolation; `app_runtime` gets only
+  SELECT/INSERT/DELETE, still constrained by tenant RLS. The function owner gets
+  only schema USAGE, identity columns `issuer`, `subject`, `user_id`, and its
+  SELECT-only policy; executor gets only private-schema USAGE and function
+  EXECUTE. Ordinary roles must not get lookup-function execution or executor
+  membership.
+- [ ] Provision a dedicated trusted authentication DB login with membership in
+  the executor role, and set its URL only as API-side
+  `GOOGLE_IDENTITY_LOOKUP_DATABASE_URL`. It must be a different principal from
+  `DATABASE_URL` (the application rejects the same host/port/user/database
+  principal). Use TLS and secret storage. Do not grant the executor role to
+  general `app_runtime`, browser, or tenant roles.
+- [ ] Verify allowed exact identity lookup through the trusted backend; confirm
+  `app_runtime` cannot execute the lookup function and its table operations
+  remain limited to the active tenant by RLS. Use only synthetic identities.
+  Confirm identity lookup is never called before successful token validation
+  and transaction consumption.
